<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions:</b></h2>
<h3><b>Do landscape blocks need adhesive?</b></h3>
<p>Adhesive is not always required, especially for dry-stack systems used in smaller projects. However, construction adhesive may be used for taller or structural walls to improve stability. The need depends on wall height, design, and load-bearing requirements.</p>
<h3><b>How deep should the base be for landscape blocks?</b></h3>
<p>The base depth depends on the size of the wall and soil conditions, but it typically requires a compacted gravel foundation that provides strong support. Deeper bases are needed for taller or load-bearing walls to prevent shifting and ensure long-term durability.</p>
<h3><b>Can landscape blocks be installed without concrete?</b></h3>
<p>Yes, many landscape block systems are designed for dry installation using compacted gravel bases instead of concrete. These are suitable for small to medium projects, but larger retaining walls may require additional structural reinforcement.</p>
<h3><b>Are landscape blocks good for retaining walls?</b></h3>
<p>Yes, landscape blocks are commonly used for retaining walls because they are strong, durable, and designed to handle soil pressure when installed correctly. Proper drainage and leveling are essential for long-term stability and erosion control.</p>

<h2><b>Final Thoughts</b></h2>
<p>Installing landscape blocks successfully depends on proper preparation, accurate leveling, and effective drainage planning. These factors determine whether the structure remains stable and durable over time or develops issues like shifting and erosion.</p>
<p>While DIY installation can work well for smaller decorative projects, larger or structural retaining walls often require professional expertise to ensure safety and long-term performance. Combining hardscape elements with thoughtful landscape design creates outdoor spaces that are both functional and visually appealing.</p>
